    "#text",
    "\n  body { margin: 0; background: #202020; }\n  <local-path> One flat, strongly-coloured backdrop, so each filtered box's interior is a\n     single colour Chrome computed — the expected value is read off Chrome's\n     own PNG rather than recomputed from the same maths as the renderer. */\n  #panel { position:relative; width:400px; height:160px; background:#c04020; }\n  .fx { position:absolute; top:50px; width:60px; height:60px; }\n  #gray  { left:10px;  backdrop-filter: grayscale(1); }\n  #inv   { left:90px;  backdrop-filter: invert(1); }\n  #sat   { left:170px; backdrop-filter: saturate(0.1); }\n  <local-path> Two functions in one list, so the composition order is exercised too. */\n  #both  { left:250px; backdrop-filter: grayscale(1) brightness(1.6); }\n  <local-path> Authored as a percentage. Chrome normalizes it to sepia(0.8) before the\n     snapshot, which is worth knowing: a computed value never reaches the\n     parser as a percentage, so this box exercises sepia rather than %. */\n  #pct   { left:330px; backdrop-filter: sepia(80%); }\n",
